    "## Q1(A) - BFS Synchronization via Transition Monoid\n",
    "*(Caelan and Alex)*\n",
    "\n",
    "**Agorithm Design**\n",
    "\n",
    "The synchronization check uses a breadth-first search over the transition monoid.Each letter $a \\in \\Sigma$ induces a transformation $\\tau_a : Q \\to Q$, and composing these corresponds to reading sequences of letters. The DFA is synchronizing if and only if the monoid contains a *constant transformation* - one that maps every state to a single state. Each transformation is represented as a tuple of images over $Q$ in sorted state order, so $\\tau_a$ becomes `tuple(tau[(q, a)] for q in states)`. This fixed ordering means that two transformations with the same action on $Q$ always produce identical tuples, making the `seen` set deduplication correct. On each step, the current transformation $f$ is composed with each generator $\\tau_a$ via\n",
    "`compose(f, t_a)`, and any composition not yet in `seen` is enqueued. BFS explores level by level, guaranteeing that if a constant transformation exists at depth $k$\n",
    "it is found at depth $k$, naturally yielding the shortest reset word. If the queue is exhausted without finding one, `False` is returned."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 4,
   "id": "e51dcd1c",
   "metadata": {
    "execution": {
     "iopub.execute_input": "2026-03-22T19:28:07.939821Z",
     "iopub.status.busy": "2026-03-22T19:28:07.939610Z",
     "iopub.status.idle": "2026-03-22T19:28:07.959303Z",
